WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:12.530
PAUL JAY: Welcome back to Reality Asserts
Itself. I'm Paul Jay on The Real News Network.

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:17.330
We're continuing our series of interviews
with John Kiriakou, the former CIA officer.

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:22.520
And in the last episode, we left on a cliffhanger.
So if you didn't watch the last episode, you'd

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:26.210
better go back and watch. In fact, you probably
should watch all the preceding episodes, 'cause

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:27.860
we're just going to pick it up.

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:28.119
Thanks for joining us again.

00:00:28.119 --> 00:00:29.710
JOHN C. KIRIAKOU: Thanks for having me.

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:31.169
JAY: Alright. So what happened next?

00:00:31.169 --> 00:00:35.260
KIRIAKOU: Well, as part of my job with the
Senate Foreign Relations Committee, like everybody

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:40.219
else on the committee staff, I had lunch on
a regular basis with foreign diplomats. And

00:00:40.219 --> 00:00:44.530
during those lunches, we would talk about
whatever happened to be in the news--Israeli

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:50.710
elections, developments in Turkey, Yemen is
collapsing, whatever happened to be interesting.

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:55.379
And we would just give each other our views
on these news events.

00:00:55.379 --> 00:01:01.050
So I was having lunch with a Japanese diplomat
at his invitation at a restaurant on Capitol

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:05.710
Hill. And toward the end of the conversation,
he said to me, so what's next for you? And

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:09.820
I said, well, I'm actually thinking of resigning.
I promised Senator Kerry I'd give him two

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:14.890
years. It's been two and a half. I miss working
for myself. I think I'm going to move on.

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:15.680
JAY: To do what?

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.820
KIRIAKOU: I wanted to go back into consulting
again. And he got very excited and he said,

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:27.820
no, don't do that; I can give you money if
you give me information. And I said, do you

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:32.250
have any idea how may times I've made that
pitch? Shame on you for even saying something

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:34.500
like that. I'm going to have to report this.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:39.670
Well, I went directly from the lunch to the
office of the Senate security officer, and

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:45.880
I said, I was just pitched by a foreign intelligence
officer. So he asked me to write him a memo,

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:51.300
and he would send the memo to the FBI. So
I wrote a very detailed memo about the lunch,

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:52.610
and it went to the FBI.

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:57.200
And then, two days later, two FBI agents came
up to the Senate security office to interview

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:03.230
me. And they said, what we want you to do
is to call him back, invite him to lunch,

00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:07.830
and try to get him to tell you exactly what
information he's looking for and how much

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:12.390
money he's willing to pay for it. And we're
going to have two agents at the next table

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:15.800
to listen in on the conversation. I said,
terrific.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:22.800
So I called him, I invited him to lunch, and
we went to the lunch, and we had another great

00:02:23.519 --> 00:02:29.810
conversation, and he told me what he was interested
in. I should add the FBI didn't show up to

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:34.069
sit at the next table. Right before that lunch,
they called and said, just go ahead and do

00:02:34.069 --> 00:02:35.579
it and write us at the memo.

00:02:35.579 --> 00:02:37.969
JAY: So who's setting you up? The FBI or the
CIA?

00:02:37.969 --> 00:02:43.109
KIRIAKOU: Well, I thought that I was helping
out the FBI by helping them identify a foreign

00:02:43.109 --> 00:02:45.620
intelligence officer.

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:50.099
So I did this a second time, and a third time,
and a fourth time. And I might have even had

00:02:50.099 --> 00:02:51.510
a fifth lunch with him.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:52.930
Finally, he told me that--.

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:55.060
JAY: But are you thinking this is a setup?

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:59.519
KIRIAKOU: No. No. I thought this was a counterintelligence
investigation.

00:02:59.519 --> 00:03:05.680
So, finally he tells me, I'm being transferred
to Cairo. I said, okay, good luck, nice to

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:09.719
meet you, nice to know you, and I never saw
him again.

00:03:09.719 --> 00:03:16.719
It wasn't until a year later that we learned
that there was no Japanese intelligence officer,

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:22.909
there was no Japanese diplomat. He was an
FBI agent. And what they were trying to do

00:03:22.909 --> 00:03:28.340
was to set me up on a real espionage charge--not
one of the bullshit espionage charges that

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:34.090
they ended up putting on me--all of which
were dropped, I should add. They knew that

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:40.290
their espionage case against me was weak to
nonexistent. I hadn't committed espionage.

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:44.620
In fact, one of the espionage charges--I'm
going to interrupt myself here--one of the

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:51.620
espionage charges was based on the fact that
I had leaked top-secret, compartmented information

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:56.709
about a CIA program to The New York Times.
Do you know what that information was? That

00:03:56.709 --> 00:04:02.540
the CIA had a program to capture or kill al-Qaeda
members.

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:03.959
JAY: Come on!

00:04:03.959 --> 00:04:08.109
KIRIAKOU: I got an espionage charge for that.

00:04:08.109 --> 00:04:11.980
The other two espionage charges were based
on the fact that I had given a former CIA

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:18.230
colleague's business card--and he's no longer
in the CIA, and he was never undercover--I

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:23.100
had given this business card to two reporters,
one at ABC and one at The New York Times.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:24.310
So I got three espionage charges.

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:27.280
JAY: Wait a sec. If you're not undercover,
there's lots CIA agents--

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:27.710
KIRIAKOU: Precisely.

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:30.400
JAY: --that are officers that are known and
that get talked about.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:34.819
KIRIAKOU: In fact, when we raised this in
court, my attorney told the judge, your honor,

00:04:34.819 --> 00:04:40.009
this man was never undercover, and all my
client did was to pass his business card,

00:04:40.009 --> 00:04:44.449
which he's handing out to everybody, to these
two reporters. And the judge said, I find

00:04:44.449 --> 00:04:49.479
that very hard to believe. So she turns to
the prosecutors and she says, is that true?

00:04:49.479 --> 00:04:55.669
And the prosecutor says, well, Your Honor,
Mr. Kiriakou really should have been more

00:04:55.669 --> 00:05:00.990
careful. And she turns to my attorneys and
says, I'll entertain him a motion to dismiss

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:06.090
those charges. So they were all eventually
dropped. But they knew, the FBI knew that

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:11.080
these charges and the espionage case against
me was very weak. So what they wanted to do

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:18.080
was to catch me actually committing espionage.
And so they sent in this pretend Japanese

00:05:19.509 --> 00:05:19.909
diplomat.

00:05:19.909 --> 00:05:24.530
JAY: Okay. You didn't fall for it, but they
continued to target you anyway.

00:05:24.530 --> 00:05:29.379
KIRIAKOU: Right. But, see, all this time,
I didn't know that I was being targeted. When

00:05:29.379 --> 00:05:36.379
I had lunch with the Japanese diplomat, this
was 2011. This was the spring of 2011. I wasn't

00:05:36.479 --> 00:05:38.289
arrested until 2012.

00:05:38.289 --> 00:05:45.289
And what really did me in was in January 2012,
I got a call from the FBI. Hi, this is so-and-so

00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:52.860
with the FBI field office in Washington. Do
you remember that case you helped us out with

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:58.800
about a year ago? I said, sure; about the
foreign diplomat? And he said, yeah. I said,

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:02.680
yeah, sure, I remember. He said, well, we've
got a similar case and we really need your

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:08.669
help. Can you come down here? I said, sure.
In fact, I said, anything for the FBI. Well,

00:06:08.669 --> 00:06:12.840
come on down on Thursday. I said, terrific.

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:18.740
So I go down on Thursday and meet with these
two FBI agents in a secure vault, a vaulted

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:25.740
conference room. And we're an hour into this
meeting before I realize, wait a minute, this

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:31.000
case is against me. I had no idea.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:36.009
But I should have known, because another thing
happened that same week. The previous week,

00:06:36.009 --> 00:06:43.009
my mother's sister died in Western Pennsylvania.
And so I rented a car and I went to the funeral.

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:50.220
Well, it turned out I also had a hearing in
Ohio, at family court in Ohio, on child support.

00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:57.430
So I rented this car, I went to my aunt's
funeral. And then from the funeral in Pennsylvania

00:06:57.430 --> 00:07:04.430
I drove to Warren, Ohio, and I checked into
a hotel to wait for the hearing on Monday

00:07:04.599 --> 00:07:05.539
afternoon.

00:07:05.539 --> 00:07:10.379
So, Monday morning I didn't have anything
to do. I got up and I thought, I'm going to

00:07:10.379 --> 00:07:15.159
go look at my grandparents' old house. I haven't
seen it in 15 years. I'm going to drive through

00:07:15.159 --> 00:07:19.930
the neighborhood and see what it looks like.
So I drive up to the house, and I'm going,

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:25.930
like, 2 miles an hour. And I notice there's
a Toyota behind me. So I waved an apology

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:27.780
and I sort of pulled over to the side of the
road.

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:28.659
JAY: And they don't want to pass.

00:07:28.659 --> 00:07:32.580
KIRIAKOU: They didn't want to pass. And I
thought, well, that's odd. So I drive up to

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:37.379
the intersection and I make this great big
U-turn in the intersection. Well, this guy

00:07:37.379 --> 00:07:43.930
makes great big U-turn as well. And I thought,
alright, it's either he's a carjacker or he's

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:47.520
a private eye that my ex-wife hired to follow
me.

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:53.030
So I know the neighborhood well. I cut through
the neighborhood, using what I would have

00:07:53.030 --> 00:07:59.189
called a surveillance detection route (I already
knew that I was being followed). And I drive

00:07:59.189 --> 00:08:05.590
to the cemetery where my grandparents are
buried. So I stop the car near their graves.

00:08:05.590 --> 00:08:10.590
I get out. This guy passes me and parks about
100 feet away and turns around, and he's just

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:11.280
watching me.

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:12.680
JAY: Yeah, they want you to know.

00:08:12.680 --> 00:08:15.460
KIRIAKOU: Right. It was pretty clear.

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:22.460
So I called my wife and I said, this is going
to sound crazy, but I'm under surveillance.

00:08:23.009 --> 00:08:27.860
And she agreed with me that it was probably
my ex-wife having hired a private eye--to

00:08:27.860 --> 00:08:30.710
catch me doing what, I had no idea.

00:08:30.710 --> 00:08:31.770
But then from there--.

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:32.800
JAY: [incompr.] wouldn't be so obvious.

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:33.860
KIRIAKOU: Right.

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:39.539
From there I drove to the Packard Museum.
Remember the car, the Packard, from the '40s

00:08:39.539 --> 00:08:44.039
and '50s? Well, it was made in that town,
Warren, Ohio. And there's a Packard Music

00:08:44.039 --> 00:08:45.959
Hall, and next to it is the Packard Museum.

00:08:45.959 --> 00:08:50.519
So I drove into the parking lot of the Packard
Museum and I made a U-turn there. He did the

00:08:50.519 --> 00:08:55.360
same. I waited until the light was red, and
then I sped through the red light, and he

00:08:55.360 --> 00:09:01.940
got caught up in traffic. And then I just
sort of wended my way back to the motel.

00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:07.730
So I thought, well, some local yokel private
eye just got too close and didn't know what

00:09:07.730 --> 00:09:12.279
to do 'cause I was driving erratically, and
I didn't think about it. Well, it was after

00:09:12.279 --> 00:09:17.370
this meeting with the FBI four days later
that I realized it was the FBI. They had followed

00:09:17.370 --> 00:09:22.910
me all the way from Virginia. They probably
thought I was running to Canada or something,

00:09:22.910 --> 00:09:28.130
because their call had tipped me off. I didn't
know.

00:09:28.130 --> 00:09:31.510
But I realized that next Thursday, in that
meeting, that I'm in deep trouble. In fact,

00:09:31.510 --> 00:09:37.149
at the end of the meeting, they said, well,
you should know that as we speak, we're executing

00:09:37.149 --> 00:09:43.420
a search warrant on your home. And I said,
am I under arrest? And he said, not yet. And

00:09:43.420 --> 00:09:45.079
I said, well, I want to talk to my attorney.

00:09:45.079 --> 00:09:49.690
JAY: Triggered by what, according to them?
This meeting with this Japanese--phony Japanese

00:09:49.690 --> 00:09:50.100
diplomat?

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:53.209
KIRIAKOU: They decided they couldn't charge
me with that, which they couldn't, 'cause

00:09:53.209 --> 00:09:57.250
I hadn't done anything wrong. And they went
ahead and charged me with three counts of

00:09:57.250 --> 00:10:04.250
espionage related to the--saying that the
CIA was capturing al-Qaeda leaders, and the

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:09.990
two business cards. So I got three espionage
charges there. They added a charge of making

00:10:09.990 --> 00:10:15.000
a false statement to the CIA's Publications
Review Board, which was absurd. That was in

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:18.610
the process of writing my book. They said
that I made a false statement.

00:10:18.610 --> 00:10:22.180
I don't even recall what the false statement
was supposed to have been. But I remember

00:10:22.180 --> 00:10:27.660
sitting in the conference room with my lawyers,
and they said, so what exactly was the false

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:33.240
statement? And I said, I don't know, I don't
know what this is supposed to be. I was supposed

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:37.690
to have lied to them somehow. Anyway, they
also charged me with one count of violating

00:10:37.690 --> 00:10:42.560
the Intelligence Identities Protection Act
of 1982.

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:44.199
JAY: The business card.

00:10:44.199 --> 00:10:49.540
KIRIAKOU: No, it was different, because that
guy was never undercover. I had gotten a call

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:55.990
in 2008--so this is four years earlier--I
had gotten a call from a reporter who was

00:10:55.990 --> 00:11:01.810
writing a book on the rendition program, and
he wanted to know if I knew anybody that I

00:11:01.810 --> 00:11:06.010
can introduce him to that he could interview
for the book. I said, I don't know anybody.

00:11:06.010 --> 00:11:10.500
I didn't have anything to do with rendition.
I really never met any of those guys. So he

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:16.190
said, well, what about the guy in your book
that you mention running into at the airport

00:11:16.190 --> 00:11:23.190
in Pakistan? And I said, yeah, I can't remember
his name. And he said, I think his first name's

00:11:23.699 --> 00:11:28.010
John. And I said, oh, right, that's John Doe
or John Smith. I said, I don't know where

00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:33.850
he is. He's probably retired and living somewhere
in Northern Virginia. That was a felony, and

00:11:33.850 --> 00:11:36.139
I served two years in prison for that conversation.

00:11:36.139 --> 00:11:39.060
JAY: That's the one you actually had to plead
to.

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:41.019
KIRIAKOU: Yes.

00:11:41.019 --> 00:11:46.320
So they tried hard to get me. And when I say
hard, I mean they tried to get me 45 years.

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:53.320
JAY: So that's the short of the--to jump to
the chase, you were facing all these espionage

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:53.600
charges,--

00:11:53.459 --> 00:11:53.709
KIRIAKOU: Right.

00:11:53.690 --> 00:11:55.630
JAY: --and so you plead to this one.

00:11:55.630 --> 00:12:00.490
KIRIAKOU: My investigator developed information
indicating that the reporter did not get that

00:12:00.490 --> 00:12:05.860
name from me. He got it from a disgruntled
former CIA officer in Bethesda, Maryland.

00:12:05.860 --> 00:12:07.399
JAY: So why didn't you fight it?

00:12:07.399 --> 00:12:11.320
KIRIAKOU: Well, we went to the prosecutors
to fight it, and the prosecutors said, first

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:14.290
of all, you can't prove that we withheld the
information, you can't prove that we ever

00:12:14.290 --> 00:12:19.899
had the information. Secondly, we'll drop
that charge, we'll drop that IIPA charge.

00:12:19.899 --> 00:12:22.100
But we're going to go after you on the three
espionage charges.

00:12:22.100 --> 00:12:25.970
JAY: But why--they're so ridiculous, the three
espionage charges. Why didn't you fight them?

00:12:25.970 --> 00:12:30.300
KIRIAKOU: Because this is the Eastern District
of Virginia. It's called the "Espionage Court".

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:34.800
And nobody ever wins in the Eastern District
of Virginia. This is why I told Ed Snowden

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:39.610
when he first went public, don't come home,
because you won't get a fair trial. Your jury's

00:12:39.610 --> 00:12:46.009
going to be made up of FBI agents, military
officers, CIA officers. You're never going

00:12:46.009 --> 00:12:48.610
to get a fair hearing here. Never.

00:12:48.610 --> 00:12:54.980
So for me it ended up being an economic question:
can my family live without me for two years,

00:12:54.980 --> 00:13:01.029
basically on the kindness of strangers and
family members? And the answer was yes. But

00:13:01.029 --> 00:13:05.620
could they live without me for 20, which is
what I realistically could have expected if

00:13:05.620 --> 00:13:10.949
I had been convicted of the espionage charges?
No, they couldn't have made it for 20. So

00:13:10.949 --> 00:13:12.560
I cut my losses and I took a plea.

00:13:12.560 --> 00:13:13.639
JAY: It's extortion.

00:13:13.639 --> 00:13:17.910
KIRIAKOU: Mhm. But this is what the Justice
Department does. What they do is they heap

00:13:17.910 --> 00:13:23.019
charges on you, knowing that they're going
to drop all of them but one if you take a

00:13:23.019 --> 00:13:27.880
plea, because what you're eventually going
to do is you're going to be so beaten down

00:13:27.880 --> 00:13:32.230
and you're going to spend every cent you have
and every cent you've been able to borrow

00:13:32.230 --> 00:13:36.639
from friends or family members, you're going
to spend your pension--unless they take it

00:13:36.639 --> 00:13:40.120
away from you like they did in my case--and
you're going to still have these charges.

00:13:40.120 --> 00:13:41.300
JAY: They took your pension away from you.

00:13:41.300 --> 00:13:47.019
KIRIAKOU: Yeah. I lost my federal pension.
Mhm. I've got nothing. I'm going to have to

00:13:47.019 --> 00:13:52.800
work till the day I die. There's nothing I
can do about it.

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:56.480
So what they do is they heap on these charges,
knowing that eventually you're going to give

00:13:56.480 --> 00:14:02.500
up and you're going to take a plea. That's
why, according to ProPublica, that actually

00:14:02.500 --> 00:14:09.500
studied this, the feds have a 98.2 percent
conviction rate. I said in a speech right

00:14:09.660 --> 00:14:14.130
before I went to prison, Saddam Hussein won
98 percent of his last presidential election

00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:18.709
and we said the fix was in. The Justice Department
wins 98.2 percent of their cases and we say

00:14:18.709 --> 00:14:21.930
they're geniuses. I say there's something
wrong with the system.

00:14:21.930 --> 00:14:27.810
JAY: Now, this was all designed to send a
message in the CIA: do not--if you're one

00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:33.100
of us, you don't even poke around at all.
And it sounds--the more you tell the story,

00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:37.130
it's--almost sounds like it's less about that
you went public on torture, which I'm sure

00:14:37.130 --> 00:14:40.339
pisses them off at the time, but the fact
that you keep poking.

00:14:40.339 --> 00:14:41.230
KIRIAKOU: Right.

00:14:41.230 --> 00:14:44.769
JAY: And number two, to shut you up. This
has not shut you up.

00:14:44.769 --> 00:14:45.480
KIRIAKOU: Oh, no.

00:14:45.480 --> 00:14:46.639
JAY: In fact, quite the opposite.

00:14:46.639 --> 00:14:50.620
KIRIAKOU: No, quite the opposite. In fact,
I said, again, in a speech before I went to

00:14:50.620 --> 00:14:55.800
prison, if they thought this would shut me
up, they don't know me at all, because now

00:14:55.800 --> 00:14:59.259
I've devoted my life to fighting them.

00:14:59.259 --> 00:15:01.250
JAY: Thanks for joining us.

00:15:01.250 --> 00:15:02.430
KIRIAKOU: Happy to be here.

00:15:02.430 --> 00:15:07.040
JAY: And thank you for joining us on Reality
Asserts Itself on The Real News Network. And

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:14.040
I'm sure John's going to be back on The Real
News, not too distant future.

